WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.392
Serverless functions: Vercel Functions as the example.

2
00:00:03.672 --> 00:00:09.453
A serverless function is code the platform runs on demand per request and scales automatically.

3
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:12.677
You deploy a handler; the platform handles servers.

4
00:00:12.957 --> 00:00:15.901
The limit that matters most is maximum duration.

5
00:00:16.181 --> 00:00:33.141
Vercel’s limits page (with Fluid compute, the default for new projects) gives a default of 300 seconds on every plan; Hobby can’t go higher, while Pro and Enterprise can configure up to 800 seconds, with a 1,800-second maximum in beta.

6
00:00:33.421 --> 00:00:46.115
The page is explicit that the duration includes time spent sending the response, including streamed responses, and that a function that runs out of time gets a 504 FUNCTION_INVOCATION_TIMEOUT.

7
00:00:46.395 --> 00:00:57.061
Functions on Vercel’s separate Edge runtime have a different rule: they must begin sending a response within 25 seconds and can then stream for up to 300 seconds.

8
00:00:57.341 --> 00:01:00.584
Two other details on that page are LLM-relevant.

9
00:01:00.864 --> 00:01:01.675
The 4.

10
00:01:01.955 --> 00:01:06.989
5 MB request and response body limit does not apply to streamed responses.

11
00:01:07.269 --> 00:01:19.067
And Vercel bills Functions by active CPU time plus provisioned memory time, where waiting for I/O, such as calling AI models, does not count toward active CPU time.

12
00:01:19.347 --> 00:01:26.600
What this means for you: a five-minute ceiling, by default, on everything a request does, retries and fallbacks included.

13
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.123
Short chat replies and extraction calls fit comfortably.

14
00:01:30.403 --> 00:01:38.445
A multi-step agent that sometimes runs for eight minutes does not, and should be a background job (lesson 1) regardless of host.
